## §0 What is being falsified

SAEOS-1 §9/A8 carries the programme's load-bearing risk:

> "Ω1 (self-population) is presented as a breakthrough but assumes PAEOS emits
> clean structured decisions. Does it?" **[MODIFY]** Partially. PAEOS *produces*
> all the required artifacts […] but as prose documents, not as typed emission
> bundles. Slice S1 (§8) exists precisely to build and test that extraction.
> **Carried:** if L15 artifacts prove too unstructured to extract losslessly, S1
> fails and Ω1's value is overstated. **This is the specification's single
> largest untested assumption.**

Ratified as D7 (§10): "Accepted, scoped: S1 is explicitly a falsification test."
S1's exit criterion (§8): *"A sealed PAEOS goal produces a valid bundle;
round-trip is lossless."*

This probe executes that test **by hand, before building the extractor** — three
real sealed goals, hand-extracted into the §4.3 node types and the §4.2 envelope,
scored per-field.

**The falsification claim under test.** A8 fails — NO-GO — if the prose exhaust
of a sealed goal cannot yield the §4.3 node types at a fidelity sufficient for
S2's single method (`tradeoff_precedents`), i.e. if `Decision`, `Option`
(including rejected), and `TradeOff` are not recoverable.

